1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,051
Singapore's First Chief Minister:

2
00:00:03,131 --> 00:00:08,350
What David Marshall Could
and Couldn't Do, 1955 to 1956

3
00:00:08,350 --> 00:00:10,921
At 5 p.m. on the 7th of June 1956,

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:15,600
David Marshall resigned as
Singapore's first Chief Minister,

5
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:20,044
fourteen months after taking
office and three weeks after

6
00:00:20,124 --> 00:00:24,725
constitutional talks in London
had ended without agreement.

7
00:00:24,725 --> 00:00:28,284
He had said he would resign
if he could not bring home

8
00:00:28,364 --> 00:00:31,800
full internal self-government,
and he kept to that.

9
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:35,666
The Straits Times of the following
morning put the news across

10
00:00:35,746 --> 00:00:39,167
the top of its front page
under the headline "Lim told:

11
00:00:39,247 --> 00:00:43,575
take the helm," and turned at once
to the man who would succeed him.

12
00:00:43,575 --> 00:00:47,452
The story of those fourteen
months is as much about what

13
00:00:47,532 --> 00:00:51,700
the office could not do as
about what Marshall did with it.

14
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:55,251
David Marshall was born
in Singapore on the

15
00:00:55,331 --> 00:00:59,304
12th of March 1908 into
a Baghdadi Jewish family

16
00:00:59,384 --> 00:01:03,100
and was called to the
bar in London in 1938.

17
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:06,791
He joined the Singapore
Volunteer Corps that

18
00:01:06,871 --> 00:01:10,300
year and was mobilised in December 1941.

19
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:14,712
After the fall of Singapore he
was marched to Changi on the

20
00:01:14,792 --> 00:01:18,976
17th of February 1942 as a
prisoner of war and was later

21
00:01:19,056 --> 00:01:23,700
sent to Japan, where he worked
in the coal mines of Hokkaido.

22
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:26,442
He returned to Singapore in

23
00:01:26,522 --> 00:01:30,075
February 1946 weighing 107 pounds.

24
00:01:30,075 --> 00:01:35,973
After the war he became one of
Singapore's best-known criminal lawyers,

25
00:01:36,053 --> 00:01:38,331
earning an average of 11,300

26
00:01:38,411 --> 00:01:42,200
Singapore dollars a
month in the early 1950s.

27
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:45,815
In August 1954 he founded the Labour Front

28
00:01:45,895 --> 00:01:49,150
with Lim Yew Hock and Francis Thomas.

29
00:01:49,150 --> 00:01:53,803
In March 1955, a month before
the election, he left his law

30
00:01:53,883 --> 00:01:58,375
firm after disagreements over
his political involvement.

31
00:01:58,375 --> 00:02:02,654
The election of the 2nd of
April 1955 was the first

32
00:02:02,734 --> 00:02:07,269
held under the Rendel
constitution, which enlarged the

33
00:02:07,349 --> 00:02:12,050
Legislative Assembly to 32
members, 25 of them elected.

34
00:02:12,050 --> 00:02:15,850
The rest were officials
and nominated members.

35
00:02:15,850 --> 00:02:20,398
The British kept defence, external
affairs and internal security

36
00:02:20,478 --> 00:02:24,600
in their own hands, and the
Governor held reserve powers.

37
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:29,012
There were more than
300,000 registered voters,

38
00:02:29,092 --> 00:02:33,982
and the Straits Times
reported that 160,395 of them,

39
00:02:34,062 --> 00:02:37,025
53 per cent, went to the polls.

40
00:02:37,025 --> 00:02:40,045
The Sunday Times of the
3rd of April ran the

41
00:02:40,125 --> 00:02:43,920
headline "Labour wins, Marshall
will be Chief Minister"

42
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,100
over a report of a
"sensational left swing."

43
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:49,767
The Labour Front had won ten seats,

44
00:02:49,847 --> 00:02:53,850
the most of any party but
well short of a majority.

45
00:02:53,850 --> 00:02:56,817
The Straits Times of
the next day headed its

46
00:02:56,897 --> 00:02:59,865
front page "All say,
no coalition" while the

47
00:02:59,945 --> 00:03:03,200
Labour Front's leaders
met behind closed doors.

48
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:06,745
Marshall nevertheless
formed a minority government

49
00:03:06,825 --> 00:03:09,725
with the Alliance and nominated members.

50
00:03:09,725 --> 00:03:12,932
Three colonial officials,
the Chief Secretary,

51
00:03:13,012 --> 00:03:18,300
the Financial Secretary and the
Attorney-General, also sat in the cabinet.

52
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:22,430
Marshall held the Commerce and
Industry portfolio himself,

53
00:03:22,510 --> 00:03:26,575
and Lim Yew Hock became
Minister for Labour and Welfare.

54
00:03:26,575 --> 00:03:29,412
His government was barely a month old when

55
00:03:29,492 --> 00:03:32,200
the Hock Lee bus strike turned violent.

56
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:35,517
The dispute began when
the bus company dismissed

57
00:03:35,597 --> 00:03:37,853
workers for belonging to a union,

58
00:03:37,933 --> 00:03:42,250
and students from Chinese middle
schools joined the strikers.

59
00:03:42,250 --> 00:03:45,215
On the 12th of May 1955 riots

60
00:03:45,295 --> 00:03:48,550
broke out along Alexandra Road.

61
00:03:48,550 --> 00:03:52,413
Four people died, among
them two police officers,

62
00:03:52,493 --> 00:03:58,125
an American journalist and a 16-year-old
student, and 31 were injured.

63
00:03:58,125 --> 00:04:01,870
The Straits Times of the 13th
of May carried Marshall's

64
00:04:01,950 --> 00:04:06,400
statement that the trouble was
"a bid to destroy my Government."

65
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:10,247
An agreement to end the
strike was reached on the 14th

66
00:04:10,327 --> 00:04:14,102
of May after Marshall met
union leaders and advisers,

67
00:04:14,182 --> 00:04:18,400
including Lee Kuan Yew, Fong
Swee Suan and Lim Chin Siong.

68
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:22,613
After students staged sit-ins
at several Chinese schools,

69
00:04:22,693 --> 00:04:26,229
he reopened the closed
schools once an all-party

70
00:04:26,309 --> 00:04:30,075
committee on Chinese
education had been appointed.

71
00:04:30,075 --> 00:04:32,600
Critics described his handling of the

72
00:04:32,680 --> 00:04:35,425
strike as an "unconditional surrender."

73
00:04:35,425 --> 00:04:39,875
The second test was over the
limits of the office itself.

74
00:04:39,875 --> 00:04:44,440
In July 1955 Marshall asked
for four junior ministers,

75
00:04:44,520 --> 00:04:47,100
and the Governor approved two.

76
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:50,153
He threatened to resign,
and the dispute was

77
00:04:50,233 --> 00:04:54,782
resolved in August after the
Secretary of State for the Colonies,

78
00:04:54,862 --> 00:04:57,425
Alan Lennox-Boyd, visited Singapore.

79
00:04:57,425 --> 00:05:01,425
The outcome enlarged the
Chief Minister's powers.

80
00:05:01,425 --> 00:05:05,950
Marshall's government passed a
good deal in its fourteen months.

81
00:05:05,950 --> 00:05:10,343
The Emergency Regulations
were replaced by seven bills,

82
00:05:10,423 --> 00:05:14,491
including the Preservation
of Public Security Bill,

83
00:05:14,571 --> 00:05:18,475
and formally lifted on
the 29th of October 1955.

84
00:05:18,475 --> 00:05:23,084
The all-party committee on education
produced a white paper that

85
00:05:23,164 --> 00:05:28,000
set out a policy of teaching in
English, Chinese, Malay and Tamil.

86
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,622
A Malayanisation Commission,
set up in August 1955,

87
00:05:32,702 --> 00:05:36,494
issued an interim report in February 1956,

88
00:05:36,574 --> 00:05:42,475
and Marshall began weekly
Meet-the-People sessions in June 1955.

89
00:05:42,475 --> 00:05:47,750
The Central Provident Fund
was amended in May 1955.

90
00:05:47,750 --> 00:05:51,975
In February 1956 the Assembly
voted unanimously to allow

91
00:05:52,055 --> 00:05:56,742
members to speak in languages
other than English, and a select

92
00:05:56,822 --> 00:06:01,586
committee on multilingualism
tabled its report on the practical

93
00:06:01,666 --> 00:06:06,125
arrangements on the 7th of
June 1956, the day he resigned.

94
00:06:06,125 --> 00:06:10,628
A Free Legal Aid Bill was still
under discussion when he resigned,

95
00:06:10,708 --> 00:06:13,337
with the Singapore Bar Committee having

96
00:06:13,417 --> 00:06:16,750
sent a memorandum on it
to the Attorney-General.

97
00:06:16,750 --> 00:06:22,159
The all-party delegation, thirteen
strong, left for London on the

98
00:06:22,239 --> 00:06:27,475
14th of April 1956, and the talks
opened on the 23rd of April.

99
00:06:27,475 --> 00:06:31,539
Marshall asked for full
self-government by April 1957,

100
00:06:31,619 --> 00:06:37,375
and the British offered a more limited
measure of internal self-government.

101
00:06:37,375 --> 00:06:39,929
The sticking point was the council

102
00:06:40,009 --> 00:06:42,875
that would control internal security.

103
00:06:42,875 --> 00:06:46,737
The British proposed equal numbers
from Britain and Singapore,

104
00:06:46,817 --> 00:06:48,899
with the British High Commissioner

105
00:06:48,979 --> 00:06:51,650
in the chair and holding the casting vote.

106
00:06:51,650 --> 00:06:54,852
Marshall proposed a
chairman born in Singapore

107
00:06:54,932 --> 00:06:57,992
or the Federation of
Malaya and appointed by

108
00:06:58,072 --> 00:07:01,425
the Federation government,
and no casting vote.

109
00:07:01,425 --> 00:07:03,975
The British rejected both proposals,

110
00:07:04,055 --> 00:07:08,796
citing concern about communist
influence and unrest in the colony,

111
00:07:08,876 --> 00:07:12,675
and the talks reached a
deadlock on the 15th of May.

112
00:07:12,675 --> 00:07:17,265
The Straits Times of the 16th of
May, in a special 8 a.m. edition,

113
00:07:17,345 --> 00:07:19,953
ran the headline "The talks fail" with

114
00:07:20,033 --> 00:07:23,925
the words "Marshall ('I'll
resign') returns on Sunday."

115
00:07:23,925 --> 00:07:28,708
Marshall made a last attempt to
restart the negotiations on 16

116
00:07:28,788 --> 00:07:33,650
and the 17th of May, which the
rest of the delegation opposed.

117
00:07:33,650 --> 00:07:36,865
On the 7th of June
Marshall moved a motion in

118
00:07:36,945 --> 00:07:40,527
the Legislative Assembly
asking members to endorse

119
00:07:40,607 --> 00:07:43,975
the delegation's rejection
of Britain's terms.

120
00:07:43,975 --> 00:07:47,780
The Liberal Socialist Party
proposed an amendment declaring

121
00:07:47,860 --> 00:07:52,455
that the British offer should have
been accepted as an interim measure,

122
00:07:52,535 --> 00:07:56,025
because it was a considerable
constitutional advance.

123
00:07:56,025 --> 00:07:59,536
The amendment found a
single supporter outside the

124
00:07:59,616 --> 00:08:03,270
party's own ranks and was
defeated by 18 votes to 6,

125
00:08:03,350 --> 00:08:06,725
and the Assembly then
backed Marshall's motion.

126
00:08:06,725 --> 00:08:09,195
Two hours after the Assembly rose,

127
00:08:09,275 --> 00:08:12,650
he submitted his
resignation to the Governor.

128
00:08:12,650 --> 00:08:17,411
The Straits Times editorial the
next day said that his resignation

129
00:08:17,491 --> 00:08:22,178
"comes as close to anti-climax as
resignation could ever be," and

130
00:08:22,258 --> 00:08:27,025
reported that the Governor had
invited Lim Yew Hock to take over.

131
00:08:27,025 --> 00:08:31,894
On the same front page, the paper
reported that a member of the Assembly,

132
00:08:31,974 --> 00:08:35,500
Seah Peng Chuan, had resigned
from the Labour Front.

133
00:08:35,500 --> 00:08:39,850
Lim Yew Hock became Chief
Minister on the 8th of June.

134
00:08:39,850 --> 00:08:44,007
Lim Yew Hock led a delegation to
London in March and April 1957,

135
00:08:44,087 --> 00:08:47,648
and the talks of the 11th of
March to the 11th of April

136
00:08:47,728 --> 00:08:51,686
produced an agreement giving
almost complete self-government,

137
00:08:51,766 --> 00:08:55,327
with the British keeping
external security and internal

138
00:08:55,407 --> 00:08:59,564
security shared through a council
of three members from Britain,

139
00:08:59,644 --> 00:09:03,550
three from Singapore and one
from the Federation of Malaya.

140
00:09:03,550 --> 00:09:07,985
The State of Singapore
Act received royal assent

141
00:09:08,065 --> 00:09:12,030
on the 1st of August
1958, and the People's

142
00:09:12,110 --> 00:09:16,625
Action Party won 43 of
the 51 seats in May 1959.

143
00:09:16,625 --> 00:09:20,664
Marshall stayed in the
Assembly as a backbencher and

144
00:09:20,744 --> 00:09:25,100
founded the Workers' Party
on the 7th of November 1957.

145
00:09:25,100 --> 00:09:29,409
He lost his Cairnhill seat
in 1959, won the Anson

146
00:09:29,489 --> 00:09:35,141
by-election in 1961 and left the
Workers' Party in January 1963,

147
00:09:35,221 --> 00:09:39,700
losing his seat as an
independent later that year.

148
00:09:39,700 --> 00:09:45,737
From 1978 to 1993 he served as
Singapore's ambassador to France,

149
00:09:45,817 --> 00:09:48,875
Portugal, Spain and Switzerland.

150
00:09:48,875 --> 00:09:53,175
He died on the 12th of December 1995.

151
00:09:53,175 --> 00:09:57,309
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Singapore's road to

152
00:09:57,389 --> 00:10:02,150
self-government is usually told
through the years after 1959.

153
00:10:02,150 --> 00:10:05,602
The fourteen months before
them show a first elected

154
00:10:05,682 --> 00:10:09,541
government working out what
an elected government inside a

155
00:10:09,621 --> 00:10:13,277
colony could actually decide,
and how far the Governor,

156
00:10:13,357 --> 00:10:17,025
London and the Assembly's
own parties would let it go.

157
00:10:17,025 --> 00:10:20,767
The internal security council
that failed in 1956 was

158
00:10:20,847 --> 00:10:24,660
settled on different terms
the year after, with a seat

159
00:10:24,740 --> 00:10:28,626
for the Federation of Malaya,
and the disagreement over

160
00:10:28,706 --> 00:10:32,600
that council was what ended
Marshall's time in office.
